messages:
sam: Hey team — running into issues using the Node/TS SDK inside a Next.js Edge function. I tried a normal import and got \"require is not defined\" and some fs/buffer errors. Quick checklist for ESM vs CJS, bundlers (Next/Vite) and edge compat?

julia: Short version: the SDK ships CJS + ESM + an edge/browser build. Bundlers sometimes pick the wrong entry (CJS) which pulls in Node-only stuff. For edge you must use the edge/browser entrypoint.

mike: On Next 13 edge runtime what's the import path? I tried `import Redwood from 'redwood'` and it pulled CJS.

julia: We expose a dedicated edge entry — import from `redwood/edge` (or the package's exports \"edge\" entry). Example:
```
import { createClient } from 'redwood/edge'
const client = createClient({ apiKey: process.env.RW_KEY })
```
Make sure package.json has an `exports` map pointing edge -> ./dist/edge/index.mjs so Next/Vite can resolve it.

mike: For Vite I keep hitting `global is not defined` when bundling the SDK. Any Vite-specific tips?

anya: For Vite: avoid prebundling the CJS variant. Common config we use internally:
```js
// vite.config.js
import { defineConfig } from 'vite'
export default defineConfig({
  ssr: { noExternal: ['redwood'] }, // force using ESM source
  define: { global: 'globalThis' }
})
```
Also `optimizeDeps.exclude` on older setups can help. This forces Vite to use the ESM files instead of a CJS rollup.

kim: Streaming gotcha for edge — Node's stream.Readable won't work. Use WHATWG streams and `Response` for streaming in edge functions. Example:
```js
// edge-friendly streaming
const stream = new ReadableStream({
  start(controller) { controller.enqueue(new TextEncoder().encode('hi'));
                 controller.close() }
})
return new Response(stream)
```
Our edge SDK helpers return a Web ReadableStream (not a Node stream) if you import the edge build: `client.streamCompletion()` -> ReadableStream.

sam: Good to know. Is there a recommended package.json layout for libs/apps to support both CJS and ESM users?

julia: If you need dual support, use conditional exports. Example snippet we recommend for reference:
```json
{
  "main": "./dist/cjs/index.js",
  "module": "./dist/esm/index.js",
  "exports": {
    ".": {
      "require": "./dist/cjs/index.js",
      "import": "./dist/esm/index.js",
      "edge": "./dist/edge/index.mjs"
    }
  }
}
```
Then new ESM apps can use `type: \"module\"` and import the esm path, older CJS apps still get require support.

mike: One more — Next Edge doesn't support `process.env` at runtime like Node. Any hints for secrets in edge?

anya: Correct — edge functions get inlined envs at build time. For runtime secrets use a server-side API (non-edge) or fetch a token from an internal service. Also avoid Node builtins (fs, net, Buffer); prefer globalThis/TextEncoder/ReadableStream.

ci-bot: deploy-check: ran edge-smoke with the edge build + streaming test on node18-workers — streaming ok ✅

sam: Thanks all — I'll switch to `redwood/edge` import + update vite config and report back if still blocked. :thumbsup:
